LOGBOOK.md:181:- GATE: A reviewer-owned unfiltered streak passed 16 consecutive 427-test runs, including historical hang positions 13 and 16, then run 17 exited 1 because `channelOperationPressureIsBounded` observed `.cancelled` instead of `.resourceLimitExceeded`. The unchanged pressure test reproduced independently after nine focused passes; focused run 10 exited 1 with the same mismatch. The mandatory 20-run aggregate gate therefore remains failing even though the cancellation and keepalive cases passed in the failed aggregate run.
LOGBOOK.md:244:- PHYSICAL RESULT: On the designated arm64 Mac15,9 running macOS 26.5/Xcode 26.5, the rebuilt archive passed 49 inside-out inspection assertions and nine negative drift cases. Ten start/message/stop cycles plus host termination exited 0 with one manager and no provider process; exact-path user-Applications uninstall/reinstall, PlugInKit rediscovery, and a final cycle also exited 0. No probe crash report or privacy finding was present.
LOGBOOK.md:416:- ROOT CAUSE: r4 settled `macos.provider` with **no keychain row and no file exception** because `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` grants `/Library/Keychains` read/write to any process holding `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension`. Every observation was taken on **macOS 26.5 (25F71)**. The binding generated-target floor is **macOS 15.0** (`.spec/platform-distribution.md`, ADR-016/3r0993). A base sandbox grant is a property of the **running system**, not of this contract — so the matrix asserted, for nine major releases it never read, something true only of the one it did. Verdict 08 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:433:- ROOT CAUSE: the matrix has carried a prose `consumers` list since r1 and it was **never compared against the board's dependency graph**. Transitive closure over every task and bug record: 19 declared consumers, **16 reachable, 3 not** — `TASK-260715-1o9wjz`, `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`, `TASK-260715-29ws8l`. With `max_parallel=1` the scheduler was free to run all three while the matrix sat in `analysis` under its **eighth** changes-requested verdict, authoring the macOS credential resolver, the snapshot loader and the profile-trust contract against an unaccepted contract.
LOGBOOK.md:435:- FINDING: four more elements carrying obligations were absent from `consumers` entirely, three assigned from inside **prose fields** — `legacy.migrationDecisionOwner` named `TASK-260715-35nc5m` for eight revisions, two sentences named `TASK-260717-xempiv` for five. Hence D1's derived set scans **every string** in the contract, not three named lists.
LOGBOOK.md:478:- ROOT CAUSE: r5 moved eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left `crossPlatformRules[2]` still asserting the **r4** contract — the shared Keychain access group granted to `macos.host`. The r5 validator passed **846 checks** because not one of its 31 rules looked at the summary. The generalisable defect is not the sentence: it is that a summary *asserting* a grant is a **second, unsynchronised statement** of data that lives elsewhere, and nothing in a per-row validator can see the contradiction. Reviewer verdict 05 F1.
LOGBOOK.md:483:- DECISION: the corrected Keychain sentence states the exclusion **universally** ("to NO macOS target on either channel, in either family") rather than naming the macOS host as excluded, which is a deliberate divergence from the reviewer's literal rework text. **Naming a target is what went stale**: r4 named the host as a grantee, r5 moved the row, the name stayed. A universal clause is derived from all eight rows and has nothing to forget. Recorded in the JSON as `whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed` rather than left as an unexplained difference.
LOGBOOK.md:484:- ROOT CAUSE (F2, and the more dangerous of the two): r5 corrected `TASK-260728-q5kjta` — the **human ceremony** — and left `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, the **unattended task that spends that authorization**, on the pre-r5 contract. Correcting an authorization node does not correct the node that consumes it. Its AC2 required the packet-tunnel entitlement "**only on provider identifiers**", which contradicted **r1**, not just r5: Apple requires `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has enforced it on all eight target rows since r2. Provider-only verification would have *passed* a portal state in which neither macOS host App ID carries the capability — surfacing at first build, after the sitting was over.
LOGBOOK.md:486:- VERIFICATION: **975 checks** exit 0 (r5: 846); **117 negative gates** exit 0 (r5: 92), 25 new — 18 against F1 including a stale grantee appended *after* an intact grant clause, which a substring check alone would miss; 5 against the P1 pin; 2 against the rationale document. **53/53 r5 preservation assertions** hold against a pre-edit copy: all eight target rows byte-for-byte, exactly two top-level keys added, none removed. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. The reviewer's own `jq` gate flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.
LOGBOOK.md:538:- VERIFICATION: 27 rules / 598 checks, exit 0, and **41 negative gates** (was 23) each re-introducing a defect and failing closed naming its rule — nine for the missing/mis-scoped exception, five for X1, four that delete or empty an assertion. That last group exists because the failure being fixed was an assertion set that did not cover its own contract; R27 now pins the assertion ids so the set cannot silently shrink again.
LOGBOOK.md:573:- VERIFICATION: `validate_matrix.py` runs 18 rules / 247 checks over the JSON contract, exit 0; nine deliberate mutations (broken containment, legacy-prefixed identifier, wrong NE value, granted pending-keychain row, unprefixed macOS group, collapsed probe group, C1-authorized distribution profile, iOS marked provisioned, sysex-install on the provider) each fail closed with the expected rule.
LOGBOOK.md:612:- ANOMALY: `task-board validate` reported one dependency cycle, but an independent DFS over all 397 elements found **eight**. The validator stops at the first cycle it reaches, so a clean-looking single-cycle report can hide a cluster. Always re-traverse the live graph after a cycle fix rather than trusting the next validate run alone.
LOGBOOK.md:723:- VERIFICATION: Nine focused Swift Testing cases pass five repeated runs and ThreadSanitizer; the full suite passes 328 tests. Strict Swift format, core-boundary and privacy/prohibition scans, relay protocol schema/vector/conformance drift checks, and Swift build pass.
LOGBOOK.md:837:- VERIFICATION: Eighteen admission tests cover below/at/above every independent ceiling, 128/256-way reserve races, 500 release/open and 500 late-callback races, 250 explicit-release/deinit ownership races, ID exhaustion, queued-byte overflow, session-health races, 8,000 sustained pressure rejects alongside 2,000 admitted-flow progress steps, every terminal reason across 320 churn lifecycles, and exact local/exported baseline recovery. The bounded-queue convergence probe passes 20/20 runs; focused normal and TSan admission/diagnostics suites pass; `make validate-core` passes boundary/native gates, 306 tests in 27 suites, and the post-test build; strict recursive Swift format, privacy/boundary, diff, and board validation pass.
LOGBOOK.md:1296:- DECISION: Pull-request and merge-queue work is credential-free, read-only by default, and unable to reach protected environments, production secrets, privileged runners, publication permissions, or trusted release cache/artifact namespaces. All nine future blocking checks for the macOS working-client path must be runnable on GitHub-hosted macOS; optional relay-native Linux evidence remains separately owned and non-blocking for that path. The current seed's generic `validate` job on Ubuntu is an observed migration input, not a Linux architecture requirement. Every third-party action is full-SHA pinned.
LOGBOOK.md:1297:- DECISION: Nine stable checks block default-branch integration. Release preparation binds an exact protected-branch commit and fresh evidence into one immutable candidate; relay staging, Developer ID/notarization, Sparkle signing, asset publication, and feed publication use separate protected environments and credentials under one non-cancelling production concurrency group.
LOGBOOK.md:1302:- REVIEW REWORK 2: A later independent review found three binding inconsistencies: raw `task-board validate` was fail-open for a reported status issue, Linux runner scope conflated generic validation with relay hardening, and the evidence said eight checks while the contract named nine. The contract now requires `TASK-260715-2hef52` to ship a JSON-parsing production wrapper plus invalid-status negative fixture, separates the current Ubuntu validation seed from relay-native Linux evidence, makes every macOS-path blocking check runnable on GitHub-hosted macOS, and consistently binds nine checks. A fresh exact-head reviewer verdict is required; human ratification remains decoupled at `TASK-260717-2d308k`.
LOGBOOK.md:1352:- VERIFICATION: Nineteen focused Python tests include the exact raced foreign-destination inode, pathname-to-symlink archive replacement, direct archive symlink, compressed-size and hostile-PAX metadata bounds, and injected `fdopen` leak checks. Deterministic accepted-archive regeneration, bundle/provider graph, both SwiftPM adapters, unsigned workspace/macOS products, core/protocol gates, formatting, and the broad 450-test Swift suite pass with the existing 25 known issues.
LOGBOOK.md:1372:- VERIFICATION: The exact fresh-build failure is retained in `.temp/TASK-260715-mocqmr/native-darwin-arm64-current/report.json`. After the fix, eight repeated fixture signal checks and twelve consecutive full native Darwin arm64 gates passed with exit 130. The pinned four-target build and manifest verification, Go tests/vet, release-tool regressions, actionlint, supply-chain audit, toolchain check, and 84%+ affected Python coverage pass.
LOGBOOK.md:1380:- FINDING: Existing double-build executable and protocol-test bytes were identical, but Syft 1.48.0 emitted a wall-clock `creationInfo.created` value and random UUID suffix in `documentNamespace` for each SPDX file. Those eight metadata differences cascaded into different four-entry manifest and checksum bytes, while the comparator checked only executables and incorrectly passed.
LOGBOOK.md:1401:- VALIDATION ANOMALY: A post-rework full `swift test` run exited `1` because the unrelated libssh2 `clientAndServerRekey` M0 gate did not observe `.closed` before its assertion; the run otherwise reached all 455 tests and reported the existing 25 known ReluxNIOSSH issues. An immediate exact `mandatoryGate` rerun passed all 18 argument rows (exit `0`, nine known unavailable-adapter issues), so the failure is retained as a transport-suite timing anomaly rather than attributed to the UI-test infrastructure.
LOGBOOK.md:1407:- VERIFICATION: Six focused fake-only Swift suites passed 53 tests. The real coordinator boundary rejects every invalid profile class before injected Keychain/network fakes; all nine `SSHHostKeyDecision` cases traverse the candidate-neutral transport seam with `hostPolicy -> credentialLookup -> authentication` only on accepted paths. One independently owned lifecycle registry retains concrete task, connection, observer, and callback tokens across all 16 bounded iterations; gated cancellation observes all four non-zero, and balanced teardown restores the same registry baseline after success, every rejecting host decision, an injected thrown policy, and cancellation without assigning counters to zero. Three additional cleanup/redaction matrix repetitions passed; captured-log prohibited-data, test-source live-Security, public-fixture, live-network, and self-reset-counter scans returned no matches. Coverage reported 87.75% affected lines and 87.04% affected functions; strict Swift format, core boundaries, and diff checks exited 0. The first focused compile exited 1 on a missing explicit `return`; the corrected rerun and every subsequent gate exited 0. No real Keychain, SSH connection, NetworkExtension runtime, VPN configuration, route, DNS, interface, or packet-filter operation ran.

